WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.420
Detection practices help find use-after-free defects earlier, but they work best when code already has clear lifetime contracts.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:17.240
Compiler warnings, static analysis, runtime sanitizers, assertions, boundary tests, and code review all contribute different signals.

3
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.580
None of them replaces ownership discipline.

4
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:32.420
Compiler warnings may reveal suspicious lifetime or control-flow patterns: returning local addresses, using uninitialized pointers, ignoring cleanup results, or following confusing branches after release.

5
00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:39.320
A warning near allocation, deallocation, callback registration, or cleanup deserves careful review.

6
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:48.960
Static analysis can help identify stale references, inconsistent cleanup, ownership confusion, and paths where a pointer may be used after release.

7
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:58.580
It can follow aliases across functions and notice when one path releases storage while another path still treats the pointer as live.

8
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.460
Runtime sanitizers can detect use-after-free during testing.

9
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:13.520
They are most valuable when tests exercise failure paths, repeated operations, resizing, teardown, callback cancellation, and cleanup after partial initialization.

10
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:19.300
A sanitizer finding should lead to a fix and a regression test that protects the same lifetime contract.

11
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.180
Assertions can document lifetime assumptions during development.

12
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.720
They can state that an object is initialized before use, not destroyed before an operation, or owned by the current component before release.

13
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:38.580
Assertions should support internal invariants, while external input and runtime failures still need ordinary validation and error handling.

14
00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.820
Code review should trace ownership from allocation through every transfer and release.

15
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:56.400
Reviewers should ask who owns the object, who borrows it, who can store it, when it is released, what aliases remain, what happens on error, and whether later code can still reach stale references.

16
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.540
That trace is the core of use-after-free prevention.
